<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danjong of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1441">1441</a>–<a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Danjong, Yi Hong-wi, Prince Nosan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Danjong (personal name Yi Hong-wi) was the sixth king of Joseon, who came to the throne at the age of 11 on the death of his father, King Munjong, in 1452. As he was too young to rule, the state was governed by the councillors Hwangbo In and Kim Chongsŏ, until his uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejo-of-joseon">Grand Prince Suyang</a>, overthrew them in a coup in 1453. Danjong was forced to abdicate in 1455; after a plot by six officials to restore him failed the next year, he was demoted to Prince Nosan and exiled to Yeongwol, and in November 1457 he was put to death on the orders of Suyang, now King Sejo, though the records conflict on how he died. He was restored to his title, with the temple name Danjong, only in 1698.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Danjong, whose personal name was Yi Hong-wi, was born on 18 August 1441 during the reign of his grandfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a>. His mother, Crown Princess Gwon, did not recover after giving birth and died the next day. His father, King Munjong, was sickly, and Danjong acceded to the throne at the age of 11 when Munjong died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Because the king was too young to rule, government fell to the Chief State Councilor Hwangbo In and the Left State Councilor Kim Chongsŏ, with Princess Gyeonghye as his guardian. In 1453 his uncle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejo-of-joseon">Grand Prince Suyang</a>, led a coup d'état after persuading a number of scholars and officials who had served in Sejong's court to support his claim; Hwangbo In and Kim Chongsŏ were killed in front of the gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>.</p><p>In late January 1454 the 14-year-old king married the 15-year-old daughter of Song Hyeon-su of the Yeosan Song clan, later known as Queen Jeongsun. In 1455 he was forced to abdicate and became king emeritus, and his wife became Queen Dowager Uideok. The next year six court officials tried to restore him, but the plot was discovered and they were executed at once. Danjong was demoted to Prince Nosan and exiled to Cheongnyeongpo in Yeongwol, and his wife lost her status and was sent out of the palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sejo was at first hesitant to execute his nephew and showed mercy, but came to see Danjong as a continuing threat and, on the advice of the court, ordered him put to death in November 1457. The records conflict. The Veritable Records of the Joseon Dynasty say Danjong hanged himself after his sympathisers were executed, with Sejo not directly responsible. During the reign of King Seonjo a senior official recounted that a royal inspector poisoned him on Sejo's orders, and an 18th-century work, the Yeollyeosil Gisul, describes a court attendant strangling him with a cord attached to a bowstring.</p><p>It is said that Sejo ordered the body thrown into a river and decreed that anyone who gave it a proper burial would be executed; Eom Heung-do, the hojang of Yeongwol, buried it secretly in his family grave, later known as Jangneung, and went into hiding. His act is commemorated each year in the village, where locals reenact the funeral. After 200 years, in 1698, Prince Nosan and his wife were restored, receiving the temple name Danjong and the posthumous name Queen Jeongsun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danjong of Joseon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Yeongwol County, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1451" aria-label="Gyeongbokgung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7722 812 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Danjong of Joseon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8172 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1451">What was here in 1451? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
